.svelte-kit
build
node_modules
package-lock.json

# Written by pipeline/scrapers/calendar.py through common's write_stamped_json,
# which spells the corpus's one JSON form (indent 2, real UTF-8, trailing
# newline). Prettier would reformat it and then fight the writer on every
# re-parse -- the same reason pipeline/corrections/ and pipeline/overrides/ are
# out of the hook's scope (root CLAUDE.md, "Linting"). It is an oracle read by
# tests, never source anyone edits by hand.
src/lib/calendar/oracle/

# Written by scripts/build-lectionary.mjs from the corpus's USCCB reading
# pages. Same argument as the rule above: a generated file with its own writer,
# reformatted here, is a diff on every regeneration and a fight nobody wins. It
# is committed rather than derived at build time on the precedent of
# common/book_forms.json -- a derived table that is read as source.
src/lib/lectionary/table.json

# Written by the same script, and the ORACLE the derived rules are checked
# against rather than anything the site serves -- `rules.test.ts` is its only
# reader, which is what keeps it out of every route's chunk.
src/lib/lectionary/days.oracle.json

# Written by scripts/language-coverage.mjs, which sorts its keys and its
# language lists because the baseline is read as a DIFF -- that ordering is the
# whole point of the file. Prettier collapses a short array onto one line and
# wraps a long one, so it and the writer disagree about every work with fewer
# than about eight languages, and each would undo the other on every sync.
# Same argument as the two rules above. The report is the same bytes, shipped.
scripts/language-coverage.baseline.json
static/language-coverage.json

# The rules above are all "prettier and a writer would fight". THIS ONE IS A
# DIFFERENT REASON: Cloudflare's `_headers` format is a path line then indented
# `Name: value` lines, prettier infers no parser for an extensionless file and
# says so on STDERR while exiting 0 -- which is exactly the shape
# `.githooks/pre-commit` reads as a failure, so committing an edit to this file
# was refused with a message about parsers. It went unnoticed for as long as it
# did because the file had not been touched since the hook existed.
static/_headers

# Same failure, same reason: prettier has no parser for `.txt` either, so
# editing `robots.txt` or a font licence was refused by the hook with a message
# about parsers. None of these is a format prettier has an opinion about —
# `robots.txt` is directives, `security.txt` is RFC 9116 fields, the OFL files
# are licence text that may not be reflowed at all.
static/**/*.txt
